WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.913 --> 00:00:03.663
(pleasant music)

00:00:12.282 --> 00:00:16.170
- Hi, members of the Yale
graduating Class of 2021.

00:00:16.170 --> 00:00:20.040
I'm Robert Lopez, JE Class of 1997.

00:00:20.040 --> 00:00:23.120
I'm a songwriter, best known
along with my wife, Kristen,

00:00:23.120 --> 00:00:25.440
for co-writing the song "Let it Go"

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:27.680
and all the songs from
"Frozen," "Frozen 2,"

00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:31.160
and most recently the theme
songs for "WandaVision."

00:00:31.160 --> 00:00:32.880
And I am so excited

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:35.910
to get to be this year's
Yale Class Day speaker.

00:00:35.910 --> 00:00:38.330
My time at Yale was one of
the highlights of my life.

00:00:38.330 --> 00:00:39.940
There hasn't been a day
that I haven't thought

00:00:39.940 --> 00:00:42.900
about some great person
I knew, great book I read

00:00:42.900 --> 00:00:45.690
or great memory I have from Yale.

00:00:45.690 --> 00:00:48.290
After I graduated, I moved
back home with my parents,

00:00:48.290 --> 00:00:50.630
without whose fierce support
I could never have had

00:00:50.630 --> 00:00:52.130
a career in the arts.

00:00:52.130 --> 00:00:54.300
I joined a musical theater
songwriting workshop

00:00:54.300 --> 00:00:58.160
where I met Jeff Marks with
whom I wrote the show "Avenue Q"

00:00:58.160 --> 00:01:01.910
and Kristin Anderson, who
became my wife and my work wife,

00:01:01.910 --> 00:01:04.400
my lyricist and my love.

00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:07.040
And somewhere along the way, I
co-wrote "The Book of Mormon"

00:01:07.040 --> 00:01:09.350
with Matt Stone and Trey Parker.

00:01:09.350 --> 00:01:10.560
Over the span of my career,

00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:12.750
I've been able to share in many awards

00:01:12.750 --> 00:01:15.920
but always in collaboration
with some wonderful person.

00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:20.110
So on Class Day, I plan to
talk about the saving grace

00:01:20.110 --> 00:01:22.220
of collaboration and friendship

00:01:22.220 --> 00:01:23.970
and how it can take
the weight of the world

00:01:23.970 --> 00:01:26.140
off your stressed-out shoulders.

00:01:26.140 --> 00:01:28.640
And I'll reflect a
little on my time at Yale

00:01:28.640 --> 00:01:32.960
and share a memory and
probably even a song or two.

00:01:32.960 --> 00:01:34.910
Of course, my speech will only be part

00:01:34.910 --> 00:01:36.930
of the great tradition of Class Day.

00:01:36.930 --> 00:01:39.530
I've spoken to the Class Day
committee and I'm so impressed

00:01:39.530 --> 00:01:40.980
with them and everything they're planning.

00:01:40.980 --> 00:01:42.650
It's gonna be great.

00:01:42.650 --> 00:01:45.230
So I wanna say thanks to President Salovey

00:01:45.230 --> 00:01:47.170
and everyone at Yale for
entrusting me with this.

00:01:47.170 --> 00:01:49.910
It's a huge honor, and
I'm really looking forward

00:01:49.910 --> 00:01:51.920
to seeing you on Class Day on May 23rd.

00:01:51.920 --> 00:01:52.753
Thank you.

